/* video device.                                                              */
/*                                                                            */
/* INTRODUCTION TO PARDUS                                                     */
/* ======================                                                     */
/*                                                                            */
/* Programmable Affordable Remote-controlled Drive Utility Standard (PARDUS)  */
/* is an open source hardware and software standard for driving multiple      */
/* actuators (such as stepper motors) with optional feedback devices (such as */
/* limit switches or image-based feedback from one or more cameras) and with  */
/* remote (over a network) control and script automation.                     */
/* Originally developed to use the Raspberry Pi as hardware controller and    */
/* server, the modern version uses a standard Linux computer as server for    */
/* greater scaleability.                                                      */
/* The PARDUS system is composed of three core components:                    */
/*                                                                            */
/* 1. The PARD Server                                                         */
/* 2. The PARD Daemon                                                         */
/* 3. The PARD Client                                                         */
/*                                                                            */
/* in addition to the hardware and software that drive the actuators, camera  */
/* and other feedback sensors. Pardcap is an example of this 'other software' */
/* used to 'drive' a camera.   An example of the 'other sofware and hardware' */
/* used to drive the actuators being the parduino.ino sketch for Arduino.     */
/*                                                                            */
/* The PARDUS system was first developed in 2019 and published in abstract    */
/* form at the Winter Meeting of the Pathological Society of Great Britain    */
/* and Ireland, ref: Pardus: An Affordable Open Source Hardware and Software  */
/* Robotic Platform for Standard Microscopes. Tadrous, P. J. Journal of       */
/* Pathology 250: 15. ISSN: 0022-3417; 1096-9896 2020                         */
/*                                                                            */
/* The practical application shown in that publication was remote control of  */
/* a Zeiss Standard microscope but the PARDUS system is for remote and        */
/* automated operation of motorised robotics systems in general (with an      */
/* emphasis on imaging systems like microscope stages and telescope mounts)   */
/* and not limited to motorising standard commercial microscopes. This is     */
/* evidenced by the fact that the latest version of PARDUS is used to control */
/* the DIY CNC stage module of the PUMA microscope, made entirely from        */
/* off-the-shelf components and 3D printed parts.                             */
/*                                                                            */
/* See the project Codeberg page for me details:                              */
/* https://codeberg.org/TadPath/PARDUS                                        */
/*                                                                            */
/*                                                                            */
/* P. J. Tadrous 31.01.2020 (last edit 13.09.26)                              */
/*                                                                            */
/* Cross-platform port (MS Windows via MSYS2 UCRT64 and macOS via clang)      */
/* 14.11.2026: Every function that touches an operating-system service which  */
/* differs between Linux, Windows and macOS (the camera, the TCP server       */
/* sockets used to talk to pardserver, the Ctrl+C handler, the audible beeps, */
/* millisecond sleeping and process start-up / shut-down) is now provided in  */
/* whole-function pre-processor branches:                                     */
/*                                                                            */
/*    #if IS_WINDOWS_NATIVE   ...  Windows version (Media Foundation camera,  */
/*                                 Winsock 2 sockets, waveOut beeps)          */
/*    #elif defined(__APPLE__) ... macOS version (AVFoundation camera, UVC    */
/*                                 controls through IOKit, AudioQueue beeps)  */
/*    #else                   ...  original Linux version (v4l2, POSIX        */
/*                                 sockets, PulseAudio)                       */
/*                                                                            */
/* Where macOS behaves exactly as Linux (POSIX) the same function is used for */
/* both and the branch reads  #if IS_WINDOWS_NATIVE ... #else ... #endif      */
/* All OS-neutral code, including the complete GTK4 GUI, is shared and is the */
/* same on every operating system.                                            */
/* On Windows and macOS the camera controls are presented with the V4L2       */
/* control IDs, names and (where the OS permits) units, and the six V4L2      */
/* pixel format codes are used for the image streams, so camera settings      */
/* files are exchangeable between the three operating systems (see the        */
/* 'Cross-platform notes' document for the limits of that exchange).          */
/*                                                                            */
/* 16.11.2026: (1) the "Error when retrieving frame sizes" pop-up that the    */
/* Windows (and macOS) version showed after every successful size listing     */
/* is removed (on_camset_show read a Linux-only errno convention); (2) the    */
/* Windows server socket is made dual-stack (IPV6_V6ONLY cleared), so that    */
/* pardserver's IPv4 connection to 127.0.0.1 is accepted (it was refused with */
/* Winsock error 10061), and the same option is set explicitly on macOS;      */
/* (3) LC_NUMERIC is set to "C" after GTK starts, so that numbers in settings */
/* files always use "." (the Prof's choice S1); (4) ps_recv_dc() reports a    */
/* 0-byte read (an orderly close by the peer) as a hang-up on every system    */
/* (the Prof's choice S4).                                                    */
/*                                                                            */
/* 17.11.2026: (1) the Linux server socket also clears IPV6_V6ONLY, so that   */
/* pardcap no longer depends on the Linux setting net.ipv6.bindv6only (the    */
/* Prof's choice N2); (2) update_framerate_resolutions() sets CurrDims_idx to */
/* the VGA entry when it falls back to 640x480, so that on_camset_show() no   */
/* longer clears the size selection and maxframerate[] is never read at index */
/* -1 (the Prof's choice N3; all three systems).                              */
/*                                                                            */
/*                                                                            */
/******************************************************************************/

// Version number
#define PARDCAP_VERSION "1.1.0"

// Linux:
//
// You will need to have certain developer versions of some libraries
// installed to compile this. See below for what to install. 
//
// Compile with:
//
// gcc `pkg-config --cflags gtk4` -Wall -o pardcap pardcap.c `pkg-config --libs gtk4` -lm -lpng -ljpeg -lpulse-simple -lpulse -lfontconfig
// or
// gcc -O2 -march=native `pkg-config --cflags gtk4` -Wall -o pardcap pardcap.c `pkg-config --libs gtk4` -lm -lpng -ljpeg -lpulse-simple -lpulse -lfontconfig
//
// Required Libs:
//
// You will need the developer libs for v4l2, gtk3, libjpeg and libpng.
// The audio GUI signals require the PulseAudio libraries which may 
// already come with your linux as standard but, if not, see below.
// Note: Arch linux gets the developer parts of a lib as standard with
// the lib. Other types of linux need to specifically install the dev or
// devel versions of the lib.
// Install the following packages according to the installation method
// used in your linux distro. Use the packages and versions that are
// appropriate to your architecture. The below examples are for Arch,
// Fedora, Debian and Ubuntu:
//
//  Distro:     Arch            Ubuntu/Debian          Fedora
//  ====================================================================
//           v4l-utils          libv4l-dev         libv4l-devel
//           libjpeg-turbo      libjpeg-dev        libjpeg-turbo-devel
//           gtk3               libgtk-3-dev       gtk3-devel
//           libpng             libpng-dev         libpng-devel
//           libpulse           libpulse-dev       pulseaudio-libs-devel
//
// This also uses libfontconfig but this will probably already be installed on
// your system. You will need to install it if that is not the case.

// MS Windows 10 or later (added 14.11.26):
//
// In an MSYS2 UCRT64 shell install the tool-chain and libraries once with
//
//   pacman -S mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gcc mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-pkgconf
//             mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gtk4 mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-libpng
//             mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-libjpeg-turbo mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-fontconfig
//
// and compile with (type all on one line):
//
//   gcc -O2 -Wall -o pardcap.exe pardcap.c `pkg-config --cflags gtk4 libpng libjpeg fontconfig`
//       `pkg-config --libs gtk4 libpng libjpeg fontconfig` -lmfplat -lmf -lmfreadwrite -lmfuuid
//       -lole32 -luuid -loleaut32 -lws2_32 -lwinmm -lm
//
// The camera is reached through Media Foundation (the camera controls through
// the DirectShow IAMVideoProcAmp / IAMCameraControl interfaces and kernel
// streaming), the server sockets through Winsock 2 and the beeps through the
// waveOut functions of winmm. Add -mwindows to the command to stop a console
// window opening when the program is started from Explorer (the messages that
// pardcap prints on the console are then not visible).
//
// macOS 11 (Big Sur) or later (added 14.11.26):
//
// Install the Xcode Command Line Tools (xcode-select --install) and, with
// Homebrew, the libraries:
//
//   brew install pkgconf gtk4 libpng jpeg-turbo fontconfig
//
// and compile with (type all on one line):
//
//   clang -std=gnu2x -x objective-c -fobjc-arc -O2 -Wall -o pardcap pardcap.c
//       $(pkg-config --cflags --libs gtk4 libpng libjpeg fontconfig)
//       -framework Foundation -framework AVFoundation -framework CoreMedia
//       -framework CoreVideo -framework IOKit -framework CoreFoundation
//       -framework AudioToolbox -lm
//
// On macOS the source file MUST be compiled as Objective-C with automatic
// reference counting (-x objective-c -fobjc-arc) because the camera is
// reached through AVFoundation, which is an Objective-C framework. The camera
// controls are reached with USB Video Class requests through IOKit and the
// beeps through AudioQueue. -std=gnu2x is used because the shared code uses
// a C23 feature (unnamed parameters in function definitions) that clang's
// default gnu17 mode accepts only with -Wc23-extensions warnings. (If the jpeg-turbo package is "keg-only" on your
// Homebrew installation, put  PKG_CONFIG_PATH=$(brew --prefix jpeg-turbo)/lib/pkgconfig
// in front of the command.)
// The first time pardcap opens a camera macOS asks whether the program that
// started it (for example Terminal) may use the camera. If that permission is
// refused it can be given later in System Settings > Privacy & Security >
// Camera.


