// The flags ps_Actioning_blob and ps_Client_wants_preview are also set when
// there is a client request to get the current tissue content value. 

// The 'save as' formats sent back to a client with image data use only these
// values (they are #defined in pardcapdefs.h):

// SAF_YUYV 0 // Raw YUYV 16 bpp array
// SAF_BM8  2 // 8bpp pixel array (not actually in BMP format)
// SAF_RGB  4 // 24bpp pixel array, ordered R,G,B
// SAF_BMP  5 // 24bpp pixel array, ordered B,G,R (not actually in BMP format)
// SAF_JPG  7 // Full colour JPEG file
// SAF_DOU  8 // Raw doubles array (1 channel)   } These two are only used
// SAF_DO3  9 // Raw doubles arrays (3 channels) } by the server.
// Any formatting / header adding (e.g. to bmp, png, FITS, etc.) will all be
// done at the client end (with the exception of SAF_JPG). The last two (DOU and
// DO3) are used to convey raw doubles arrays that are the result of frame
// averaging captures. The 24bpp BMP arrays are raw arrays in both cases, the
// BGR one (SAF_BMP) is NOT in word-aligned padded fashion. If any of the
// following flags are set, the image data sent back to the client will always
// be in raw doubles format: frame averaging, save as raw doubles, save as FITS.
// In the GUI local user opertion of PARD capture, these options can allow the
// saving of the image data as unsigned char (e.g. BMP, PNG) or both as an
// unsigned char format and raw doubles or FITS. In the server-client operation
// it will always and only be a raw doubles array that is transmitted and it is
// up to the client to decide if to save is at both u.char and doubles/FITS or
// just one or the other. The file name generated and transmitted back to the
// client will end with .fit if the 'save as FITS' option is enabled regardless
// of whether the 'save as raw doubles' option is also enabled (in the GUI local
// PARD capture both .fits and .dou will be saved if both those options are 
// ticked but for socket transmittal .fit trumps .dou - the data is just raw
// doubles in either case, it will be upto the receiving client to actually save
// as FITS to their local disc if they want to and they can also save a copy as
// .dou if they wish.

